// @trap memory access out of bounds
// THE UNSIGNED-LENGTH SEMANTICS, pinned as behaviour rather than described.
//
// `memory.fill`'s byte count is an unsigned i32. VL has no unsigned i32 type, so a
// caller can and will write a negative one — and -5 is 4294967291 bytes, which is
// past the end of any wasm32 memory. The instruction traps. It does NOT quietly
// write nothing, which is what a VL `while i < len` loop would do with the same
// argument, and that difference is the whole reason `std:buffer`'s `fill` and
// `copyFrom` guard `len <= 0` themselves instead of passing the caller's number
// straight through.
//
// This is deliberate at the intrinsic layer: the VL signature IS the wasm
// signature (§C2's rule, the same one the load matrix and the numeric intrinsics
// follow), so softening the length here would make the intrinsic something other
// than the instruction. Softening it is POLICY, and policy lives in std (O1 = (c)).
__memory_fill__(1024, 1, -5)
print(1)
